# M1 / B004 — rules: error-handling, frontend-framework, frontend, performance, persistence-technology, physical-structure, security, test-framework, testing.

C R62 OL API-009 | DEFAULT | Represent common expected outcomes explicitly when that improves clarity, using the simplest contract (null, bool, typed outcome, deliberately adopted Result<T>); do not force a generic Result<T> pattern; Application outcome categories describe business semantics, not HTTP-named categories; expected outcomes are not logged as application errors. | use-case outcomes | none stated
C R63 OL API-011 | MUST NOT | Do not use exceptions casually for normal control flow; do not establish custom business exceptions as the default architecture or throw and catch merely to jump between normal branches; typed exceptions for selected cases are a consistent project-level decision. | backend error flow | none stated
C R64 OL API-007 | DEFAULT | Unexpected failures propagate to centralized handling at the outer boundary (IExceptionHandler); avoid repetitive catch/log/rethrow blocks; catch only to recover, translate, perform a defined fallback, add context or clean up. | backend error flow | none stated
C R65 OL RUN-018 | MUST | Use fallback only when the fallback result is semantically acceptable; do not hide correctness failures behind convenient defaults; never swallow exceptions to continue execution. | failure handling | none stated
C R66 OL API-012 | MUST | Log an unexpected exception once at the boundary that handles it; not repeatedly across repository, Application, Controller and the global handler. | logging | none stated
C R67 OL API-002 | DEFAULT | Use ProblemDetails for non-validation HTTP failures and ValidationProblemDetails for request-validation failures; keep the machine-readable error contract stable and safe and do not make human-readable exception messages the machine-readable contract. | HTTP errors | none stated
C R68 OL API-008 | MUST NOT | Do not expose stack traces, SQL details, connection strings, internal paths, secret values, raw exception messages or other sensitive exception details in public API errors. | public API errors | none stated
C R69 OL API-021 | MUST NOT | Normal client-request cancellation is not a server failure: do not map it to a generic 500 or log it as an unhandled application error. | HTTP requests | none stated
C R70 OL API-010,DEP-010 | MUST | Validate each concern at the owning layer with FluentValidation for explicit request and use-case validation: Api transport concerns, Application use-case and workflow rules, Domain genuine invariants (not moved into validators merely because the library is available), Infrastructure external-system assumptions; no mechanical duplication across layers. | validation | none stated
C R71 OL FE-001 | DEFAULT | Use modern standalone Angular APIs for new code organized by feature; do not introduce NgModules for ordinary new feature work unless an integration or legacy dependency requires them. | frontend | none stated
C R72 OL FE-004 | DEFAULT | Use Signals for naturally synchronous local UI state and RxJS when asynchronous streams, HTTP composition, cancellation or event pipelines are clearer; avoid mechanical conversions. | frontend state | none stated
C R73 OL FE-008 | DEFAULT | Prefer functional interceptors for new code and keep interceptor responsibilities cross-cutting; do not put feature business logic in an interceptor. | frontend HTTP | none stated
C R74 OL FE-011 | DEFAULT | Avoid manual nested RxJS subscriptions; prefer composition operators when they express the behavior clearly. | frontend | none stated
C R75 OL FE-012 | MUST | When manually subscribing from Angular framework-managed code, tie the subscription lifetime to the component or service lifecycle with the project's current Angular facilities. | frontend | none stated
C R76 OL FE-007 | MUST | Frontend structure stays feature-oriented: a feature owns its UI and data-access logic; avoid application-wide technical buckets and empty architecture folders; keep core and shared small; use shared only for genuinely cross-feature primitives. | frontend structure | none stated
C R77 OL FE-002 | MUST | Keep pages and components focused on presentation and UI orchestration; do not move trivial presentation logic into services merely to make components smaller. | frontend | none stated
C R78 OL FE-003 | MUST | Keep backend communication in explicit feature data-access services (Angular: @Injectable feature services using HttpClient); do not scatter HTTP calls through presentation components; use frontend API models matching the published contract and do not import backend Domain entities. | frontend HTTP boundary | none stated
C R79 OL FE-009 | MUST | Frontend code follows the documented API error contract; centralize reusable ProblemDetails parsing when practical; do not infer business behavior from human-readable exception messages. | frontend errors | none stated
C R80 OL FE-006 | DEFAULT | Prefer simple local component or service state before introducing a state-management library. | frontend state | none stated
C R81 OL FE-005 | DEFAULT | Use route-level lazy loading for meaningful features; do not lazy-load every tiny component. | frontend routing | none stated
C R82 OL FE-010 | MUST | Use the explicit form model (Reactive Forms) for non-trivial forms when useful; frontend validation improves UX but does not replace backend validation or data-integrity and security enforcement. | frontend forms | none stated
C R83 OL RUN-001 | DEFAULT | Prefer clear, correct code first; no specialized performance optimizations without a concrete reason; prefer measurement or profiling evidence for non-trivial optimizations. | backend code | none stated
C R84 OL RUN-002 | MUST | Use async APIs for asynchronous I/O and do not block them with .Result, .Wait() or similar sync-over-async patterns. | backend code | none stated
C R85 OL RUN-003 | MUST | Propagate CancellationToken through asynchronous operations when cancellation is meaningful; no cancellation parameters on purely synchronous code without a use case. | backend code | none stated
C R86 OL RUN-004 | DEFAULT | Use TimeProvider for logic whose behavior depends on the current time and needs deterministic tests; do not mechanically replace stored or explicit timestamps. | backend code | none stated
C R87 OL RUN-005 | MUST NOT | Do not create and dispose a new HttpClient per request; use IHttpClientFactory or another appropriate long-lived strategy. | outbound HTTP | none stated
C R88 OL RUN-015 | MUST | External I/O calls have a bounded execution time appropriate to the operation; avoid overlapping timeout mechanisms whose combined behavior is unclear. | external calls | none stated
C R89 OL RUN-016 | MUST | Retry only when repeating the operation is safe; do not automatically retry state-changing operations without idempotency or another concrete duplicate-prevention mechanism. | external calls | none stated
C R90 OL RUN-017 | DEFAULT | Respect Retry-After or equivalent rate-limit guidance where appropriate; do not retry indefinitely or aggressively. | external calls | none stated
C R91 OL RUN-019 | MUST NOT | Caller cancellation is not a transient dependency failure; do not retry it or convert it into repeated outbound attempts. | external calls | none stated
C R92 OL RUN-006 | DEFAULT | Keep database work efficient: filter and project in the database when practical, avoid unnecessary round trips and over-fetching, watch for N+1 patterns, paginate potentially large result sets. | data access | none stated
C R93 OL RUN-007 | DEFAULT | Caching is an optimization, not a default architecture requirement; introduce it only for a clear measured or reasoned need with acceptable staleness semantics and choose the implementation by deployment, consistency and staleness requirements. | caching | none stated
C R94 OL RUN-023 | MUST | Every cache entry has a lifetime or another explicit invalidation strategy. | caching | none stated
C R95 OL RUN-020 | MUST | Cache keys uniquely represent the cached value and include every relevant dimension (identifier, tenant, user when user-specific, locale, query/filter parameters); avoid global keys for user- or tenant-specific data. | caching | none stated
C R96 OL RUN-021 | MUST | Invalidate or update cache entries only consistently with successful underlying mutations; the cache must not become a second source of truth. | caching | none stated
C R97 OL RUN-008 | DEFAULT | Compiled queries, ValueTask<T>, ArrayPool/MemoryPool, object pooling, custom serialization optimizations, aggressive caching and raw SQL for performance are introduced only for a measured or clearly demonstrated need; verify that allocation-oriented optimizations materially help. | backend code | none stated
C R98 OL ARC-019,ARC-015 | MUST | EF Core is an adapter-layer implementation detail: use it directly inside the adapter layer without crippling repositories to preserve a generic abstraction, and do not leak EF Core concepts (DbContext/AppDbContext, DbSet<T>, EF APIs, EF-backed IQueryable<T>, query expressions, provider types) into the use-case layer. | persistence implementation | none stated
C R99 OL ARC-018,ARC-016 | DEFAULT | A single SaveChangesAsync call is the normal transaction boundary of a use case; do not introduce explicit transaction APIs until a concrete use case requires more control. | transactions | none stated
C R100 OL PST-001 | DEFAULT | Maintain a small, deterministic, representative seed dataset for an empty Development database covering the main meaningful states and relationships, with readable deterministic examples. | Development seeding | none stated
C R101 OL PST-002 | MUST | Development seeding is an adapter-layer bootstrap concern and may use AppDbContext directly without repositories or the Unit of Work. | Development seeding | none stated
C R102 OL PST-003 | MUST | Keep substantial seed construction in a dedicated internal DbSeeder exposed through a narrow adapter-layer initialization extension, not in Program.cs, AppDbContext configuration, entity configurations or migrations. | Development seeding | none stated
C R103 OL PST-004 | MUST NOT | Automatic development seeding runs only in the Development environment; never seed Production automatically; tests own their setup and do not use the development seeder. | Development seeding | none stated
C R104 OL PST-005 | MUST NOT | The development seeder must not call Database.Migrate() or EnsureCreated() or otherwise create or upgrade the schema; migrations are applied separately before seeding. | Development seeding | none stated
C R105 OL PST-006 | MUST NOT | If representative data already exists, leave it unchanged; never use the seeder for automatic synchronization, repair, upgrade, overwrite or recreation of an existing Development database; developers recreate the local database explicitly. | Development seeding | none stated
C R106 OL PST-007 | MUST | Seed entities preserve current Domain invariants and valid relationships. | Development seeding | none stated
C R107 OL TST-007 | DEFAULT | Use the actual database provider (SQL Server) when relational or provider behavior matters (an isolated real-provider setup such as Testcontainers is optional); never claim provider fidelity from a different or non-relational provider (EF Core InMemory). | persistence tests | none stated
C R108 OL DEP-026,SEC-001 | DEFAULT | appsettings.Development.json provides a usable local Development connection string when it contains no secret credentials (e.g. LocalDB with integrated security); credential-bearing local values come from user-secrets or another approved local secret mechanism. | Development configuration | none stated
C R109 OL ARC-027 | MUST NOT | Physical structure follows actual responsibilities: no speculative projects, infrastructure, empty directory trees or generic Common/Helpers/Utils buckets; create a directory or project only when it holds meaningful cohesive content. | repository structure | none stated
C R110 OL SEC-001 | MUST NOT | Never hardcode or commit real secrets, credentials, private keys, access or refresh tokens or production connection strings in source-controlled files, including example configuration files. | all files | none stated
C R111 OL SEC-002 | DEFAULT | Use a local secret mechanism such as dotnet user-secrets for development and environment-specific secret injection or a managed secret store for deployed environments; do not assume a cloud provider not chosen. | secrets | none stated
C R112 OL SEC-003 | MUST | Treat data from outside the trusted boundary (HTTP requests, uploads, external responses, message payloads, user identifiers and filters) as untrusted and validate it at the owning layer when assumptions matter. | input handling | none stated
C R113 OL SEC-004 | MUST | Use parameterized database access; never build SQL by concatenating untrusted input (use the supported parameterization for raw SQL, micro-ORMs, ADO.NET). | data access | none stated
C R114 OL SEC-005 | MUST | Enforce authorization on the server; frontend route guards, hidden or disabled buttons and client-side checks are UX, not a security boundary. | authorization | none stated
C R115 OL SEC-006 | MUST | Anonymous access is deliberate and visible; avoid redundant [Authorize] attributes when a secure global policy already expresses the intent. | authorization | none stated
C R116 OL SEC-016 | MUST NOT | Do not implement custom token or password security, password hashing or reset-token cryptography when established ASP.NET Core authentication or Identity facilities cover the requirement. | authentication | none stated
C R117 OL SEC-017 | MUST | For Bearer/OIDC validate the properties required by the actual issuer (issuer, audience where applicable, signature/signing authority, lifetime); do not disable validation to make a token work. | authentication | none stated
C R118 OL SEC-018 | MUST NOT | Do not expose authentication-failure detail that unnecessarily reveals secrets or account state; avoid account-enumeration leaks where relevant. | authentication | none stated
C R119 OL SEC-011 | MUST | When browser cookies carry authentication credentials automatically, include CSRF protection in the design; do not assume the frontend framework removes the risk. | cookie authentication | none stated
C R120 OL SEC-012 | MUST NOT | Do not claim one browser token-storage mechanism is universally secure; choose token storage by the application's threat model. | browser token storage | none stated
C R121 OL SEC-007 | MUST | Production traffic carrying credentials or sensitive data uses TLS; respect the hosting topology (no redirection requirement when the deployment provides a secure boundary) and never disable certificate validation. | transport | none stated
C R122 OL SEC-008 | MUST NOT | Do not implement custom cryptography when a well-reviewed platform mechanism is appropriate; ASP.NET Core Data Protection suits application-generated protected payloads, not universal database encryption. | cryptography | none stated
C R123 OL SEC-009 | DEFAULT | Configure CORS to the narrowest policy satisfying actual browser-client requirements; never combine credentialed cross-origin requests with an unrestricted origin policy. | CORS | none stated
C R124 OL SEC-010 | MUST NOT | Never log passwords, access or refresh tokens, private keys, authentication secrets or full payment credentials; avoid PII without a concrete permitted need; changing the log level does not make sensitive data safe. | logging | none stated
C R125 OL RUN-014 | MUST NOT | Do not serialize arbitrary request bodies, domain objects or sensitive payloads into logs by default; log the minimum operationally useful, non-sensitive context. | logging | none stated
C R126 OL SEC-013 | DEFAULT | Security-sensitive authorization, permission or required-control checks fail safely; do not silently convert lookup or configuration failures into success. | security checks | none stated
C R127 OL SEC-014 | MUST | When a required security control cannot be configured correctly, prefer a visible startup or configuration failure over an insecure fallback. | security configuration | none stated
C R128 OL SEC-015 | MUST NOT | Do not trust client-provided file metadata such as MIME type as authoritative when upload handling or security decisions depend on actual content. | uploads | none stated
C R129 OL DEP-019 | DEFAULT | Production containers use an appropriate runtime image rather than the SDK image for convenience and do not switch to root to bypass filesystem permission problems. | containers | none stated
C R130 OL TST-001 | DEFAULT | NUnit is the default framework for .NET automated tests; in an established project deliberately using another framework follow that convention; no second test framework without a concrete reason. | backend tests | Established convention.
C R131 OL TST-002 | DEFAULT | Choose the narrowest test level that meaningfully verifies the behavior; integration tests when wiring, HTTP, persistence, serialization, authentication or component interaction matters (prefer them over reproducing real persistence or HTTP semantics with mocks); no fixed ratio. | tests | none stated
C R132 OL TST-006 | DEFAULT | Use WebApplicationFactory<Program>-based integration tests when routing, binding, middleware, authentication/authorization, DI, repositories or database persistence is what the test must prove. | integration tests | none stated
C R133 OL TST-003 | MUST | Tests verify observable behavior and survive internal refactoring; avoid depending on private methods, exact internal call sequences, specific implementation classes or incidental details; do not reproduce implementation algorithms; interaction verification only when the interaction is specified behavior. | tests | Interaction is specified behavior.
C R134 OL TST-005 | DEFAULT | For small finite input/state spaces prefer explicit declarative cases (NUnit [TestCase] rows rather than TestCaseSource generators) over loops, branches, LINQ or generated case machinery; state expected results instead of computing them. | tests | none stated
C R135 OL TST-004 | MUST | Tests are deterministic, independent of execution order and isolated from Production and Development data, uncontrolled external systems and shared mutable state; no wall-clock timing when TimeProvider can make time deterministic; never connect automated tests to Production data. | tests | none stated
C R136 OL TST-008 | MUST | Tests start from a known state and own their setup; they do not depend on development seed data or execution order. | tests | none stated
C R137 OL TST-011 | MUST NOT | Coverage is diagnostic; do not generate low-value tests merely to increase a coverage percentage. | tests | none stated
C R138 OL TST-012 | DEFAULT | Use descriptive test names communicating scenario/condition and expected behavior (e.g. MethodOrScenario_Condition_ExpectedBehavior); follow an established project convention. | tests | Established convention.

